00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
how when did you start this project when  was the first I first pitched it I don't  know when the first German the idea was  but I know that I first pitched it to  John Lasseter at Pixar in September of  2011 okay so we've been working on this  movie for the past six years okay and do  you what's what what do you now think I  know how creativity can be what do you  now think was the germ or that were what  was the catalyst that got it  past the point of an idea into let's do  this well after we finished making Toy  Story 3 I started thinking about what I  wanted to do next and I actually kicked  around a few different ideas John often  likes to have us pitch him like three  different ideas so we're not putting all  our eggs in one basket and one of those  three ideas that I pitched to John was  the idea of telling a story set against  dia de Muertos we didn't really know the  story then at that point the thing that  I pitched to him was a very different  story than we ended up making but but  from the beginning you know I was  inspired by this beautiful tradition of  dia de Muertos and the when I spent time  really learning about the details of the  tradition and how how people observe it  I discovered how central family is the  idea family to día de Muertos it's all  about family remembrance and you know  the obligation that we have to remember  our loved ones after they're gone and  most importantly to pass their stories  along to the next generation so that  their memories are never forgotten and  the more that I learned that and the  more that it kind of those ideas were  placed maybe some of my preconceptions  about what dia de Muertos was all about  the more that I realized that we had the  potential to tell a really rich story  that not only had the potential to be a  really entertaining film and really  funny colorful hopefully visually  dazzling musical but the family aspect  of it is what convinced me that the film  could have a real strong emotional core  as we're always looking for that and our  idea is something that's gonna have a  lot of emotion so who is your next  collaborator was it Darla as it was she  assigned but when you  if Darla was with me from the beginning  okay very beginning I signed myself you  assigned yourself so when you're brought  I do when you were brought on to this  project what what was it and then what  was the process of kind of taking it to  the next level and redeveloping it well  yeah we start very with teeny tiny  little teams and we sit in these rooms  for years on it and just working out the  story and and really it was Leslie  mentioned it was after we were working  on Toy Story 3 and it was Lee and I and  our head of story Jason Katz and the  next the first thing we did was we  jumped on a plane and went down to  Mexico we took many trips down to Mexico  but that trip was very very inspiring  and the most amazing and beautiful thing  was the generosity warmth kindness and  inspiration of the people we hung out  with and it was very important to leave  from early on he just kept saying I just  want to spend more time with the  families and I want to get to know their  stories and so anyway we we were  fortunate enough to to do that you  mentioned the word musical and I'm  trying to think this is a 19th Pixar  film is this like the first thing that's  the closest to a musical for pick I  think it's the closest we've had so far  because it's just wall-to-wall and it  has so many different levels of music so  you know Michael you obviously had the  score that you were worried about and  the you know tell me I wasn't so worried  about it yeah well yeah that was your  job too but it doesn't ever seem worried  but there's also the idea of bringing in  these songs and also you have a specific  culture and a culture that's layered  with many different types of music could  you talk about the beginning the music  and then bring who came in with the  partnership and how all the different  pieces fit together well you know on a  film like this when you're dealing with  songs usually the songs have to be  written first and and I also want to  mention you know the song in the film  you know remember me those two guys  aren't here tonight Robert and Kristen  Lopez who who wrote that song and I got  to give it up to them because they they  did such an amazing job with that song  but so the songs usually come first now  and scores usually last but in this case  I came in  much earlier than I would have because  it wasn't just score it was also source  music so if he's running down the street  we wanted it to feel like he was really  running down the street in in this town  in Mexico  what would you hear you know you there  be musicians playing there would be a  radio over here there would be something  going on there  we had to do all of that music so they  could animate to that so that was done  very early on and all of that music that  what we call source music was recorded  down in Mexico so we were planning this  big trip to go down to Mexico City  record with Mexico City's finest  Mexico's finest musicians do all of this  source music I was so excited about it  and then I got pulled out of that trip  because that's when this was last year's  when star wars happened and so I was  thrown onto that and I had to then  Jermaine kind of took over and headed  down to Mexico City and did an amazing  job producing all of the music that you  heard in the streets and that the bands  are playing and all of that so I I wrote  it handed it off and then there's also  some traditional music in there as well  which I know that she can speak to and  talk about that so it was that was first  then came the score after all that work  was done so Jermaine I think you're  responsible correct me if I'm wrong for  making me smile right off the bat with  the wouldn't you wish upon a star being  having a mariachi field and then you  also wrote a couple of songs with Adrian  Poco Loco and a couple so could you talk  about working as a team to try and make  this whole be a cohesive and arranging  and having him go off him to do Star  Wars and at the same time I think you  were communicating with each other you  know by Skype or whatever during that  yeah I was getting a lot of videos  jealous I was sitting on the stage  recording but I wanted to be there okay  well the logo was one of the first  things I did which was really fun to do  which was my assignment was make it  sound like symphonic Mexico and so we  you know I tried a couple versions and  that one's stuck but then I was at I did  some Arrangements on remember me with  Kristen anderson-lopez and Robert Lopez  and then eventually started writing  which was a wonderful  opportunity to and Adrienne wrote such  poetic and beautiful lyrics that it was  so easy to work with him I loved what he  sent and I found it quite genuine and  just inspired by the story we would have  all these conversations with you know  Lee Darla Adrienne and we would just  talk about what they wanted in the scene  and and I would send multiple you know  versions of demos and then come back at  notes and un poco local was one song  that we also took to Mexico and recorded  which was really fun we have the one of  the top high dojo groups called mono  Blanco it's also playing on that and  also we put some mariachis on it was  really fun to work together what I loved  about the process of recording in Mexico  is that you bring in these you know  expert musicians but but you also allow  you want the music to be inspired by the  story but you allow the story to be  inspired by the music - so instances  where in Poco Loco  Jermaine kind of in in in her demo  started adding sums up at the Auto  this-this-this footwork that she  recorded you know knockin shoes against  wooden boards for the demo and that  started to inspire like oh we could have  this we could incorporate this into the  character and and and pull Hector on  stage and have him start dancing and and  bring a little bit of that you know  Mexican folk dancing into this film and  in all of it just lends itself to a  richness and a reality and a history  that I think makes the film feel  timeless but but of a certain place and  and of a certain culture Lee talked  about the animation of skeletons which i  think is a big difference for this film  and something you guys see always seem  to have something that you tackle that  hasn't really been done before so it  seems like it would be simple but on the  other hand I understand it isn't could  you explain that a little bit sure there  wasn't any one thing on this film that  was kind of the the big you know  headline technical challenge to solve it  was more that this is a big film you  know it's  just it's it's enormous in scope and it  feels very epic actually yeah I mean  it's a relatively small story but it's  set against this this epic landscape we  had to design and build the whole land  of the Dead which was an enormous  undertaking and then populate that land  of the Dead with thousands and thousands  of skeleton characters that needed to  have clothing and like lots of things  that are you know in small numbers  aren't terribly difficult these days to  do in computer graphics but we just it  was at such a scale that that ended up  being the challenge on this but that  being said the the skeletons themselves  were a challenge there was more a design  challenge than a technical challenge we  knew they were gonna be skeleton  characters in the film from early on and  we wanted to design skeletons that  weren't scary or off-putting because in  my mind they were just characters in the  in the story they were in some cases  Miguel's own family members so we didn't  want them to be frightening in any way I  also made a choice very early on to give  them eyeballs which right that's kind of  weird very for skeletons to have eyes  but now that you've seen the think about  it too much I just knew in that scene  where Miguel and act are down in the  cenote in the cave I knew I was going to  be in a big closeup of Ector with the  camera pushing in and I needed the  audience to be emotionally connected  with him and to see him as a as a as a  person with a soul and I couldn't  imagine doing that without eyes and so  we made that decision early on and then  beyond that it was just there were  technical things about figuring out how  to you know have the skeletons be able  to break apart and come back together  and that was really hard and there was a  core group a small group of animators  that were really good at that so we  would give them the hardest shots  but yeah thank you very much

